    category: "重點關注",
    title: "Meta 商業條款重大更新：要求企業帳號授權爬取網站內容，用於 AI 訓練",
    summary: "法律專家警告，Meta 即將更新其商業服務條款（TOS），要求所有商業帳號必須授權 Meta 使用自動化軟體（如爬蟲、機器人）爬取其連結網站的內容，而不僅限於社群媒體貼文。這項條款將於 10 月 30 日生效，且企業將無法選擇退出。條款明確授權 Meta 使用這些內容來提供、開發、改進、研究、訓練和維護其產品和服務。這對新聞發行商等內容創作者尤其衝擊，因為他們可能原本可以向 Meta 收取數百萬美元的授權費用。雖然條款中未直接提及 AI，但透過「訓練」（train）一詞和爬取行為，強烈暗示了內容將用於 AI 訓練。這項變動可能改變內容版權與商業化模式，讓內容創作者必須重新評估其在 Meta 平台上的內容保護與授權策略。",
    tags: ["Meta", "商業條款", "AI 訓練", "內容爬取", "版權", "TOS"],
    title_en: "Meta Major Update to Commercial Terms: Requiring Business Accounts to Authorize Scraping of Website Content for AI Training",
    summary_en: "Legal experts warn that Meta is set to update its Terms of Service (TOS) for commercial services, requiring all business accounts to authorize Meta to use automated software (such as scrapers or bots) to crawl content from linked websites, not just social media posts. This clause will take effect on October 30th, and businesses will have no option to opt out. The terms explicitly grant Meta the right to use this content to provide, develop, improve, research, train, and maintain its products and services. This is particularly impactful for content creators, such as news publishers, who might otherwise have been able to charge Meta millions of dollars in licensing fees. Although the terms do not directly mention AI, the use of the word 'train' and the act of scraping strongly suggest that the content will be used for AI training. This change could alter content copyright and monetization models, forcing content creators to re-evaluate their content protection and licensing strategies on the Meta platform.",
    tags_en: ["Meta", "Commercial Terms", "AI Training", "Content Scraping", "Copyright", "TOS"],
    sources: [
      { name: "Ars Technica", url: "https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees", lang: "EN" }
    ]
