    summary_en: "Microsoft's newly launched Surface Laptop Ultra, featuring the Nvidia RTX Spark platform, aims to create an 'all-in-one' high-performance laptop capable of meeting both work and entertainment needs. Its target audience is professional users looking for an alternative to the MacBook Pro. The laptop starts at $2,599 and can reach up to $5,899, offering unified memory options of 24GB or 64GB. The article notes that while its Mini-LED display (3270×2180 native resolution) and excellent keyboard/touchpad design are impressive, its core challenge lies in whether users will trust the performance of the ARM system, and whether its performance can match traditional x86 systems or competing MacBook Pros under real-world workloads. Furthermore, the article suggests that users should pay attention to whether 24GB of unified memory is sufficient for the high memory demands of Windows 11, especially when gaming. Overall, while the product's hardware specifications are highly appealing, whether it can truly become the industry's 'all-in-one' laptop remains dependent on the maturity of its software and drivers.",
